Here's an example of how you can create a Java class for a main game panel:

```java
import javax.swing.*;
import java.awt.*;
import java.awt.event.*;

public class GamePanel extends JPanel implements ActionListener {
    private Timer timer;
    private int delay = 10; // Delay between each update in milliseconds

    public GamePanel() {
        setPreferredSize(new Dimension(800, 600));
        setBackground(Color.BLACK);
        setFocusable(true);
        setDoubleBuffered(true);

        timer = new Timer(delay, this);
        timer.start();

        addKeyListener(new GameKeyListener());
    }

    @Override
    public void paintComponent(Graphics g) {
        super.paintComponent(g);

        // Draw game graphics here
        // Example: g.drawRect(100, 100, 50, 50);

        Toolkit.getDefaultToolkit().sync(); // Synchronize the graphics state
    }

    @Override
    public void actionPerformed(ActionEvent e) {
        // Perform game logic and update game state here

        repaint(); // Redraw the panel
    }

    private class GameKeyListener extends KeyAdapter {
        @Override
        public void keyPressed(KeyEvent e) {
            // Handle key press events here
        }

        @Override
        public void keyReleased(KeyEvent e) {
            // Handle key release events here
        }
    }
}
```

In this example, the `GamePanel` class extends `JPanel` and implements the `ActionListener` interface. The `GamePanel` constructor sets the preferred size of the panel, sets its background color to black, and initializes timers and event listeners. The `paintComponent` method is called automatically by Swing to paint the game graphics on the panel. The `actionPerformed` method is called by the timer at regular intervals to update the game state and trigger panel repaint. The `GameKeyListener` class is an inner class that extends `KeyAdapter` and handles keyboard input.

You can use this panel in your game by creating an instance of the `GamePanel` class and adding it to your game window or frame.

