```

By wrapping your code in an `async` function, you can use the `await` keyword to wait for the import to resolve. Note that you need to call the `getImage` function for it to execute.

Here's an example of a p5.js sketch that renders a 3D cup on top of a cylinder:

First, add the p5.js and p5.js WebGL libraries to your HTML:

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
  <head>
    <meta charset="UTF-8" />
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0" />
    <title>p5.js Cup on Cylinder</title>
    <script src="https://cdnjs.cloudflare.com/ajax/libs/p5.js/1.4.0/p5.js"></script>
    <script src="https://cdnjs.cloudflare.com/ajax/libs/p5.js/1.4.0/addons/p5.sound.min.js"></script>
    <script src="sketch.js"></script>
  </head>
  <body>
  </body>
</html>
```

Then create a file called `sketch.js` and add the following code:

```javascript
let cupHeight;
let cupWidth;
let cylinderHeight;
let cylinderWidth;

function setup() {
  createCanvas(windowWidth, windowHeight, WEBGL);
  cupHeight = 100;
  cupWidth = 50;
  cylinderHeight = cupHeight / 4;
  cylinderWidth = cupWidth * 3;
}

function draw() {
  background(200);
  rotateX(frameCount * 0.01);
  rotateY(frameCount * 0.01);

  // Draw cylinder
  push();
  fill(150, 0, 0);
  translate(0, (cylinderHeight + cupHeight) / 2, 0);
  cylinder(cylinderWidth, cylinderHeight);
  pop();

  // Draw cup
  push();
  fill(0, 0, 150);
  translate(0, -(cupHeight / 2), 0);
  rotateX(PI);
  cone(cupWidth, cupHeight);
  pop();
}
```

This code snippet defines a cup and a cylinder shape using p5.js WEBGL mode. The cup is a cone shape, and the cylinder is right below the cup. The dimensions of the cylinder follow your requirements - it has a height that is a quarter of the cup height and a width three times the cup width. The shapes rotate simultaneously in the 3D space for better visualization. Adjust the values of `cupHeight` and `cupWidth` to change the size of the cup and cylinder.

Here's an example of implementing an end notify lambda function for a montage in C++ code within Unreal Engine 4:

1. Define the lambda function:

```c++
auto OnMontageEnded = [this](UAnimMontage* Montage, bool bInterrupted)
{
    if(!bInterrupted)
    {
        //TODO: Handle montage end event
    }
};
```

2. Setup the montage with the end notify:

```c++
UAnimMontage* MyMontage;
MyMontage->AddOnMontageEndedListener(OnMontageEnded);
```

3. Make sure to remove the listener when no longer needed:

```c++
MyMontage->RemoveOnMontageEndedListener(OnMontageEnded);
```

That's it! You can replace the `TODO` in the lambda with your own function to handle the end of the montage.

To create a website that looks like it's straight out of 2002, you'll want to focus on using HTML and CSS to achieve the retro aesthetic. Here's a simple example to get you started:

1. Open a text editor and create a new HTML file, let's call it `index.html`.
2. Begin by adding the basic HTML structure:

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
    <title>My Retro Website</title>
    <link rel="stylesheet" type="text/css" href="style.css">
</head>
<body>
    <!-- Website content goes here -->
</body>
</html>
```

3. Save the file and create a new file, `style.css`, in the same directory.
4. Open `style.css` and add the following CSS code:

```css
body {
    background-color: #fff;
    font-family: Verdana, Geneva, sans-serif;
    font-size: 12px;
    text-align: center;
}

a {
    color: #0066cc;
    text-decoration: underline;
}

.wrapper {
    max-width: 800px;
    margin: 0 auto;
    padding: 20px;
    text-align: left;
    background-color: #f5f5f5;
}

.title {
    font-size: 24px;
    font-weight: bold;
    margin-bottom: 20px;
}

```

5. Now you can add your website content within the `<body>` tags in the `index.html` file. For example:

```html
<body>
    <div class="wrapper">
        <h1 class="title">Welcome to My Retro Website!</h1>
        <p>This is some sample content. Feel free to modify it as you please.</p>
        <a href="#">Click me</a>
    </div>
</body>
```

6. Save both files and open `index.html` in your web browser. It should display your retro-styled website.

Feel free to modify the CSS and HTML according to your preferences to further customize the retro look.

To add a gradient background to your retro website, you can modify the CSS code in the `style.css` file. Here's an example of how you can achieve this:

```css
body {
    background: linear-gradient(to bottom right, #ffb3b3, #66ccff);
    /* Other CSS properties */
}

/* Rest of your CSS code */
```

In this example, we're applying a gradient background that goes from `#ffb3b3` (a light pink) to `#66ccff` (a light blue) using the `linear-gradient` property.

You can experiment with different colors or angles to achieve your desired retro effect.

